BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Astrophysics and Cosmology] A Birefringence-Blind Consistency Tes
 t of the Planck Cosmic Birefringence Hint — Anto I. Lonappan (UCSD)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T130000
DTSTAMP:20260925T070000Z
UID:2793@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Anto I. Lonappan (UCSD)\n\nAbstract:\nSeveral analyse
 s of Planck\, ACT\, and WMAP data report a small uniform rotation of CMB p
 olarization\, β ≈ 0.3–0.4°. If real\, this would be evidence of pari
 ty-violating physics such as axion-like fields coupled to photons. At this
  level\, however\, the signal is exactly degenerate with an overall miscal
 ibration of the detectors' polarization angles. The leading method\, the M
 inami–Komatsu (MK) likelihood\, breaks this degeneracy by using Galactic
  dust as a calibrator. That makes its detector angles\, and therefore β\,
  dependent on how the dust EB correlation is modeled.\nIn this talk\, I wi
 ll present a complementary test that never uses that foreground model. For
  any pair of maps\, the cosmic rotation cancels exactly\, leaving only the
  relative instrumental angle. We measure these relative angles from antisy
 mmetric and symmetric cross-spectra. We then combine all pairs into a gaug
 e-fixed calibration network\, with maps as nodes and pairwise angles as ed
 ges\, to reconstruct each detector's calibration offset.\nApplied to eight
  Planck NPIPE detector-set maps\, the network reconstruction agrees with t
 he MK calibration pattern (χ² = 8.18 for 7 degrees of freedom\, PTE = 0.
 32)\, with no map deviating by more than 1.8σ. I will explain what this d
 oes and does not tell us. Because both methods use the same maps\, this is
  an independent method rather than independent data. It supports the calib
 ration foundation of the β hint but cannot fix its absolute zero point.\n
 I will close by discussing how a sub-0.1° external calibration anchor\, c
 ombined with this network\, could deliver an absolute birefringence measur
 ement. This will be possible with BICEP/Keck\, SPT-3G\, the Simons Observa
 tory\, and LiteBIRD.\nAbout the Astro/Cosmology Seminar series:\nThe Ce
 nter for Dark Cosmology and Gravitation seminar series (aka\, the Depart
 ment of Physics Astrophysics and Cosmology Seminar) will showcase key dev
 elopments in astrophysics and cosmology\, the implications of these for fu
 ndamental physics\, and vice versa\, the implications of laboratory partic
 le physics discoveries for our understanding of the cosmos. Among the outs
 tanding issues in physics\, the nature of the dark matter and dark energy 
 and the origin of the supermassive black holes very early in the history o
 f the universe stand out as venues where beyond standard model physics is 
 at play or likely plays a role. The pace of discovery in this field is bre
 athtaking. The advent of gravitational wave observatories\, the James Webb
  Space Telescope\, and the Simons Observatory for the cosmic microwave bac
 kground all complement a host of laboratory experiments including LHC\, sh
 ort and long baseline neutrino oscillation experiments\, neutrino less dou
 ble beta decay searches\, and direct dark matter detection experiments\, t
 o name just a few. Moreover\, all of these research thrusts are underpinne
 d by rapid development of computational capabilities and artificial intell
 igence. The CDCG is dedicated to facilitating and bridging laboratory\, ob
 servational\, theoretical and computational studies focussed on the cosmos
 .\n\nhttps://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia
LOCATION:Mayer Room 4322
END:VEVENT
